  <div class="subsection-title">Thrips Predators/Predatory Mites.</div>
  <p>Three species of commercially available predatory mites - <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> - feed primarily on thrips, particularly flower thrips. Pollen may be an important part of the diet of these predators, allowing them to more efficiently colonize plants and provide control of pest insects.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_17">{{ source_list_17 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_18">{{ source_list_18 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Pirate Bugs.</div>
  <p>Pirate bugs (<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>) are small black and white bugs that are generalist predators of small insects (e.g., thrips, aphids, mites), and insect eggs. Many species are present in the region and they are very important natural controls of thrips and mites in agricultural fields and gardens. At least two species are sold commercially.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_19">{{ source_list_19 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Whitlefly Predator.</div>
  <p><span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is a generalist predator that will feed on whiteflies, thrips, spider mites and insect eggs. It is a member of the insect family Miridae (plant bugs) and will also sometimes feed on plants. It is used primarily to supplement other natural enemies released in tomato greenhouses.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_20">{{ source_list_20 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Spined Soldier Bug.</div>
  <p>The spined soldier bug, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, is a stink bug that is predatory on many types of caterpillars and leaf beetle larvae. Experimental work with the species is limited, although naturally occurring populations have often been reported as useful biological control agents. It occurs naturally in Colorado, along with other predatory stink bugs, but is never abundant.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_21">{{ source_list_21 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Zelus renardi.</div>
  <p>The assassin bug <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is a generalist predator that feeds on many insects in yards, gardens and agricultural fields in Colorado. It is sold in the egg stage.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_22">{{ source_list_22 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Soil Predator Mite.</div>
  <p>The soil dwelling mite, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (formerly <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>), is a generalist predator of mites and insects that spend part of their life cycle in the soil. Potential prey include fungus gnat larvae and pupae of thrips. Once introduced, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> usually can reproduce and establish with potting media.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_23">{{ source_list_23 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Stratiolaelaps scimitus:</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_24">{{ source_list_24 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (Gaelaenops gigas):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_25">{{ source_list_25 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Unspecified Soil Predator Mites:</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_26">{{ source_list_26 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Hister Beetle.</div>
  <p>Carcinopus pumilio is a species of hister beetle, which are predators of fly larvae. It is sold in the adult stage, primarily for suppression of flies in poultry rearing facilities.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_27">{{ source_list_27 }}</span></div>

  <div class="section-title">Parasites/Parasitoids of Insects</div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Trichogramma Wasps.</div>
  <p>Several species of <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> wasps exist, all of which attack and kill various kinds of insect eggs. Insect larvae already hatched are not susceptible to <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> attack. Eggs that <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> will parasitized are from insects in the order Lepidoptera (moths and butterflies), which includes cutworms, codling moths, cabbageworms and armyworms. Commercially available <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> wasps are often used as a form of a biological insecticide where they are expected to eliminate most of the developing eggs of pests shortly after release. High levels of control are not often achieved in practice, but the wasps may effectively supplement existing controls. Multiple releases of <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> wasps are recommended, since persistence of the parasites may be short-term. Several different species of <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> wasps are produced (e.g., <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>) and they have different habits. The more sophisticated suppliers will provide advice on which species is most appropriate for the intended crop and pest.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_28">{{ source_list_28 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_29">{{ source_list_29 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (Unspecified Trichogramma spp. and/or Mixture):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_30">{{ source_list_30 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Fly Parasites (Fly Predators).</div>
  <p>Several parasitic wasps develop in the pupae of filth breeding flies, including species of <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (M. raptor, M. zaraptor, M. raptorellus), <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (S. cameroni, S. endius, S. nigroarena) and <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>. These are used to suppress nuisance flies that develop on manure or other breeding sites produced by confined livestock. They are most widely marketed to suppress flies in horse facilities.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_31">{{ source_list_31 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Muscidifurax raptor:</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_32">{{ source_list_32 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Muscidifurax zaraptor:</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_33">{{ source_list_33 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Nasonia vitripennis:</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_34">{{ source_list_34 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (Unspecified flies of parasites):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_35">{{ source_list_35 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Aphid Parasitoids.</div>
  <p>Several small parasitic wasps are commercially available, primarily for control of aphids in greenhouses or interiorscapes. Some are generalists, other more specific as to the aphids they will attack. Among the most commonly available (and their hosts) are <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (green peach aphid), <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (melon/cotton aphid), green peach aphid, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (potato aphid), <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (green peach aphid), <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, and <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (green peach aphid).</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_36">{{ source_list_36 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Greenhouse Whitefly Parasitoid.</div>
  <p>A small wasp, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, attacks and develops within immature whitefly nymphs. Introduction of this parasitic wasp has proven useful for whitefly management in warm greenhouses (average temperatures above 72°F). The whitefly parasite is supplied on cards, as developing wasps within whitefly nymphs. The latter turn black when hosting this parasite.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_37">{{ source_list_37 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sweetpotato Whitefly Parasitoid.</div>
  <p>A parasite of whiteflies is <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (formerly <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>). Originally developed to help manage sweetpotato whitefly it also is an effective natural enemy of greenhouse whitefly. Adult stages may kill many developing whiteflies in the manner of a predator, by puncturing them with the ovipositor then feeding on the hemolymph/blood. Whitefly nymphs parasitized by this insect turn a golden color.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_38">{{ source_list_38 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Mexican Bean Beetle Parasitoid.</div>
  <p><span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is a small, parasitic wasp that develops within immature stages of the Mexican bean beetle. Releases should be made shortly after bean beetle eggs are first detected. This insect does not go into winter dormancy and thus rarely, if ever, survives winters.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_39">{{ source_list_39 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Mealybug Parasitoid.</div>
  <p><span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is a parasitoid of mealybugs in the genera <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span></p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_40">{{ source_list_40 }}</span></div>

</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="subsection-title">Armored Scale Parasitoid/Golden Chalcid.</div>
  <p>A small parasitic wasp, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, develops in many armored scales associated with insectarsia plants.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_41">{{ source_list_41 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Leafminer Parasitoids.</div>
  <p>Two species of parasitic wasps are used to control leafminers (<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>). <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> tends to be most efficient in warmer environments; <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> in cooler temperatures.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_42">{{ source_list_42 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_43">{{ source_list_43 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_44">{{ source_list_44 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (Mixture):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_45">{{ source_list_45 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Pathogens of Insects</div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Note:</div>
  <p>Several pathogens of insects have been commercialized. Under federal law those which are formulated to include single cell organisms (bacteria, fungi, viruses) are regulated as pesticides and their use must fully comply with label directions. Insect parasitic nematodes, also included in this section, are exempt from federal regulation.</p>

  <div class="subsection-title">Bacillus thuringiensis var. kurstaki.</div>
  <p>The kurstaki strain of the bacterium <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (Bt) is a bacterial disease organism that has been formulated into a number of microbial insecticides. Trade names include <span data-field="product_name_1">{{ product_name_1 }}</span>, <span data-field="product_name_2">{{ product_name_2 }}</span>, and <span data-field="product_name_3">{{ product_name_3 }}</span>, among others. Applied as a dust or spray to foliage, applications of this strain is effective for control of most leaf-feeding Lepidoptera - webworms, cabbageworms, leafrollers, tussock moths, etc. (Cutworms and armyworms are often less sensitive to this strain of Bt; see following product). This product is widely available at nurseries and mail order garden catalogs.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_46">{{ source_list_46 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Bacillus thuringiensis var. aizawai.</div>
  <p>The aizawai strain of the bacterium <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (Bt) also controls caterpillars (larvae of Lepidoptera). However, it is more effective than the kurstaki strain against caterpillars that are in the armyworm/cutworm family. This includes important pest species such as corn earworm, tobacco budworm, fall armyworm, and beet armyworm. Trade names include <span data-field="product_name_4">{{ product_name_4 }}</span> (Valent Biosciences) and <span data-field="product_name_5">{{ product_name_5 }}</span> (CertisUSA).</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_47">{{ source_list_47 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Bacillus thuringiensis var. israelensis.</div>
  <p>The israelensis (or H-14) strain of <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is effective for control of certain fly larvae, notably mosquitoes, black flies, and fungus gnats. (It is not effective against house flies, blow flies, shore flies and many other fly species.) Formulations sold for use as a soil drench to control fungus gnats include Knock-Out Knat and Gnats. Vectobac, Mosquito Dunks, Mosquito Rings, Bactimos Briquets are sold for use in water to control mosquitoes and black flies. Increasingly formulations to control mosquito larvae in water are available through mail order (e.g., “Knock Out Gnats”) and in some garden catalogs.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_48">{{ source_list_48 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Bacillus thuringiensis var. galleriae.</div>
  <p>The galleriae strain of <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is effective against white grub and is presently sold as the formulation grubGone!</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_49">{{ source_list_49 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Milky Spore.</div>
  <p>Milky spore is a bacterium (<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>) that is applied to soil to infect larvae of the Japanese beetle. It is used to introduce the pathogen to an area of turfgrass with Japanese beetle larvae; if it establishes, it will reproduce on its own and does not need reapplication. It has proven to provide modest suppression of Japanese beetle larvae in the Midwest, where it has long been established. It does not provide quick kill of Japanese beetle larvae following application.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_50">{{ source_list_50 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Beauveria bassiana.</div>
  <p><span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is a naturally occurring fungus disease that affects a very wide of insects - including aphids, whiteflies, psyllids, billbugs and caterpillars. Environmental conditions, particularly humidity, seem critical for the applied spores to successfully germinate and infect insects. Newly infected insects often are somewhat light brown; when the fungus sporulates it covers the insect with white spores. Available formulations are sold as Mycotrol and BotaniGard.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_51">{{ source_list_51 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Metarhizium anisopliae.</div>
  <p>Metarhizium anisopliae is a ubiquitous fungal pathogen of insects (“green muscardine”). A strain of this (F32) is marketed under the trade name <span data-field="product_name_6">{{ product_name_6 }}</span>. Granular formulations are sold to be incorporated into soil for control of certain insects with soil stages (e.g., root weevils, thrips), liquid formulations are labeled for use as a foliar spray or soil drench on ornamental plants, turfgrass, certain vegetables (e.g., onions, cucumbers, peppers, tomatoes) and small fruits (e.g., grape, raspberry, strawberry).</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_52">{{ source_list_52 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Fly Fungus.</div>
  <p>The fungus <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> is used on certain indoor crops to control aphids and whiteflies. Trade names include <span data-field="product_name_7">{{ product_name_7 }}</span>, <span data-field="product_name_8">{{ product_name_8 }}</span> and <span data-field="product_name_9">{{ product_name_9 }}</span>. Sustained high humidity is essential for effectively infecting insects. There are mixing and storage instructions with these products that are essential to consider to maintain the viability of this organism so that it can be effective. It will not grow in temperatures above 90°F (32C).</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_53">{{ source_list_53 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Nosema locustae/Grasshopper Spore.</div>
  <p>A microsporidian parasite of some grasshoppers, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, is sold as a bait formulation. It produces a fairly slow developing infection that weakens insects and usually kills them when they are molting. Adult insects are unlikely to be affected. The spores are perishable and should be used fairly soon after manufacture and/or stored with some refrigeration. M&R Durango produces the NoLo® bait formulation; Semaspore® is produced by Planet Natural.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_54">{{ source_list_54 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Parasitic (Predatory) Nematodes- Heterorhabditis species.</div>
  <p>Insect-parasitic nematodes in the genus <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> are applied to soil as a drench to control larvae of various insects. They are capable of penetrating the body of insect larvae and are the most effective from control of soil-dwelling white grubs and root weevils. They are also effective against certain soil-dwelling pests, such as fungus gnat larvae. Nematodes are available in pathogenicity to insects and sensitivity to temperature. Among those available are <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (=<span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>), <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> (e.g., Grub Stake Hi), and <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> and <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>.</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_55">{{ source_list_55 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (Heterorhabditis bacteriophora):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_56">{{ source_list_56 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Parasitic (Predatory) Nematodes - Steinernema species.</div>
  <p>Insect-parasitic nematodes in the genus <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span> are similarly applied to soil as a drench to control larvae of various insects. They are somewhat more specific in their host range and do poorly on beetle larvae, but do have a wide range that includes many pests. The most common species are <span data-field="species_name">{{ species_name }}</span>, which is used for control insects such as cutworms, thrips pupae, and fungus gnat larvae. Trade names include <span data-field="product_name_10">{{ product_name_10 }}</span> (Valent Biosciences) and <span data-field="product_name_11">{{ product_name_11 }}</span> (CertisUSA).</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_57">{{ source_list_57 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (Steinernema carpocapsae):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_58">{{ source_list_58 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Sources (Unspecified Steinernema spp.):</div>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_59">{{ source_list_59 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Insect Viruses.</div>
  <p>The commercial availability of viruses to control insects is new and they do not yet appear to be distributed through the sources of this survey. However, they can be acquired by direct contact of the manufacturers. Four present manufacturers include <span data-field="manufacturer_1">{{ manufacturer_1 }}</span>, <span data-field="manufacturer_2">{{ manufacturer_2 }}</span> (http://biotep.com/), <span data-field="manufacturer_3">{{ manufacturer_3 }}</span> (http://www.agbitech.com/) and <span data-field="manufacturer_4">{{ manufacturer_4 }}</span> (http://www.andermattbiocontrol.com/index.html) and these companies produce viruses that can be used to control 3 crop pests found in Colorado. All are allowed for use in certified organic production (OMRI listed).</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_60">{{ source_list_60 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">Codling moth Granulosis virus.</div>
  <p>CYD-X, Madex HP (Certis USA), Virosoft (BioTEPP), Madex (Andermatt Biocontrol).</p>
  <div class="source-list">Sources: <span data-field="source_list_61">{{ source_list_61 }}</span></div>

  <div class="subsection-title">NPV virus of Heliothis/Helicoverpa.</div>
